<section class="section theme">
  <div class="wrap theme__grid">
    <figure class="theme__fig theme__fig--light reveal"><img src="/images/Model_Diagram_2021-03-23.PNG" alt="Subway-map style diagram of the Strait of Georgia pelagic ecosystem model, with functional groups as stations and trophic links as coloured lines" loading="lazy"></figure>
    <div class="theme__text reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Illustration · 2021</p>
      <h2 class="display">Pelagic ecosystem model 'subway' diagram</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>For most of a decade I kept a diagram of the Scotian Shelf food web beside my desk. Later I learned that diagrams in that style are called 'horrendigrams'.</p>
        <p>I wanted a diagram for my own research but worried it would look horrendous too, so I borrowed from subway maps. Groups are stations, trophic links are coloured lines, and physical drivers from the NEMO model feed in from below. The question behind it: can an ecosystem model diagram be both appealing and informative?</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section theme theme--flip">
  <div class="wrap theme__grid">
    <figure class="theme__fig reveal"><img src="/images/screencap_codweb.png" alt="The Cod Web installation in a darkened room: quartz crystals glowing blue at the ends of fibre-optic strands hanging from a suspended canopy" loading="lazy"></figure>
    <div class="theme__text reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Installation · 2014 · stainless steel, quartz, fibre optics, electronics</p>
      <h2 class="display">Cod Web</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>My resident scholar project at the <a href="https://www.cfat.ca/">Centre for Art Tapes</a> (New Media Arts Scholarship, 2013–14) was a hanging sculpture, about 4 × 4 × 8 feet. Fibre optics ran from a hidden bank of lights to quartz crystals, which lit up in sequence following the real trophic links of the Scotian Shelf food web, from plankton to top predators. It was shown at Halifax's Nocturne: Art at Night festival.</p>
        <p>I chose quartz because crystals sit between the organic and the inorganic. They grow but they are not alive, and although they share an underlying structure, rarely are two the same. The food web matrix came from Professor Heike Lotze and Tyler Eddy.</p>
        <p><strong>Lessons learned:</strong> I don't consider it a success. I underestimated the time and experimentation needed and had to make design compromises. I was so flustered installing it by the deadline that I never captured good images of the finished piece.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section theme">
